---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageFrame from '../components/PageFrame.astro';
import Masthead from '../components/Masthead.astro';
import WorkSection from '../components/WorkSection.astro';
import ActionRow from '../components/ActionRow.astro';
import TextLink from '../components/TextLink.astro';
import Dot from '../components/Dot.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import Carousel from '../components/Carousel.astro';
import CaseLead from '../components/CaseLead.astro';
import MetaLine from '../components/MetaLine.astro';
const head = `<link rel="icon" href="data:," />`;
const afterFooter = ``;
---
<Base title={"Design work - Gorka Molero"} description={"These are screens Gorka Molero designed for four of his products, with what he decided about the look of each one."} page="design-work">
  <Fragment slot="head" set:html={head} />
  <PageFrame class="sheet">
    <Masthead />

      <CaseLead class="lead">
        <MetaLine class="meta eyebrow">2026 &middot; Product interfaces</MetaLine>
        <h2 class="case-title">Design work</h2>
        <p class="intro">I design what I build. These are screens from The Pulse, Hotpress, Koda and <em>openroadie</em>, with what I decided about the look of each one.</p>
      </CaseLead>

      <main class="systems">
      <WorkSection class="work" id="the-pulse">
        <h2><a href="./the-pulse.html">The Pulse</a></h2>
        <MetaLine class="meta">Own project &middot; 2025&ndash;2026 <Dot class="dot">&middot;</Dot> Live at <a href="https://the-pulse.games" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the-pulse.games</a></MetaLine>
        <p>The Pulse is a set of live AI-narrated interactive stories you play by voice. I set it on a near-black page with engraving-style illustrations, spaced serif capitals and one colour for each story.</p>
        <Figure class="plate">
          <div class="shot dark"><a href="./assets/design-work/pulse-home.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="./assets/design-work/pulse-home.webp" width="2560" height="1600" loading="lazy" alt="The Pulse home page: five tall panels side by side, each with an engraving-style illustration, a Roman numeral, the story's title in spaced serif capitals and its genre." /></a></div>
          <figcaption>The home page is five tall panels, one for each story, with a Roman numeral, the title and the genre over its illustration.</figcaption>
        </Figure>
        <Figure class="plate">
          <div class="shot dark"><a href="./assets/design-work/pulse-home-hover.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="./assets/design-work/pulse-home-hover.webp" width="2560" height="1600" loading="lazy" alt="The Pulse home page with the Shadow Over Innsmouth panel widened and tinted green, showing its blurb and a Begin link, while the other four panels stay dark." /></a></div>
          <figcaption>On hover a panel widens, its illustration lights up in the story's colour and the blurb and a Begin link appear, while the other four stay dark.</figcaption>
        </Figure>
        <Figure class="plate narrow">
          <div class="shot dark"><a href="./assets/design-work/pulse-story-card.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="./assets/design-work/pulse-story-card.webp" width="1240" height="1800" loading="lazy" alt="The story card for Shadow Over Innsmouth: the title over a faint illustration, a Lovecraft epigraph, the premise, and a list of ways to play, Solo, Same Screen and Invite Room, with Narration on." /></a></div>
          <figcaption>Choosing a story opens a card with the title over its illustration, an epigraph and the premise, then a ruled list of three ways to play, Solo, Same Screen and Invite Room.</figcaption>
        </Figure>
        <Figure class="plate">
          <div class="shot dark"><a href="./assets/design-work/pulse-chat.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="./assets/design-work/pulse-chat.webp" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy" alt="A story in play: serif narration in a column on the left, the player's lines right-aligned in a sans, a generated image of the stationmaster with a lantern on the right, and a What do you do? box at the bottom." /></a></div>
          <figcaption>In play, the narration is set in a serif in a narrow column, your own lines are right-aligned in a plain sans, and a generated image of the scene sits beside the text.</figcaption>
        </Figure>
        <Figure class="plate narrow">
          <div class="shot dark"><a href="./assets/design-work/pulse-phone.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="./assets/design-work/pulse-phone.webp" width="1170" height="2532" loading="lazy" alt="The Pulse home page on a phone: the five stories as a stack of wide bands with their titles over the illustrations." /></a></div>
          <figcaption>On a phone, the panels become a stack of wide bands, each title set over its illustration.</figcaption>
        </Figure>
        <ActionRow class="actions"><TextLink class="button" href="./the-pulse.html">The case study</TextLink>
          <TextLink class="button" href="https://the-pulse.games">Open the-pulse.games</TextLink></ActionRow>
      </WorkSection>

      <WorkSection class="work" id="hotpress">
        <h2><a href="./hotpress.html">Hotpress</a></h2>
        <MetaLine class="meta">Own project &middot; 2026 <Dot class="dot">&middot;</Dot> Live at <a href="https://hotpress.app" target="_blank" rel="noopener">hotpress.app</a></MetaLine>
        <p>Hotpress is an app for writing together in real time, with print-ready book and e-book export. I gave it a neobrutalist look built on 3px black borders, hard 4px shadows with no blur and a yellow accent. The screens come from a local install with a made-up manuscript, and the comment thread is a frame from a recorded demo.</p>
        <Figure class="plate">
          <div class="shot"><a href="./assets/design-work/hotpress-dashboard.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="./assets/design-work/hotpress-dashboard.webp" width="2880" height="1800" loading="lazy" alt="The Hotpress projects dashboard: a sidebar with a yellow Projects item, a yellow New Project button with a hard black shadow, and three black-bordered project cards topped by red and blue rules." /></a></div>
          <figcaption>On the projects dashboard the active menu item and the New Project button are yellow with hard shadows, and each project is a black-bordered card topped by a red or blue rule.</figcaption>
        </Figure>
        <Figure class="plate">
          <div class="shot"><a href="./assets/design-work/hotpress-editor-preview.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="./assets/design-work/hotpress-editor-preview.webp" width="2880" height="1736" loading="lazy" alt="The Hotpress editor with a manuscript, The House of Tides, and the PDF Preview panel beside it showing a typeset 6 by 9 inch page in EB Garamond with drop caps and a centred scene break." /></a></div>
          <figcaption>Beside the editor, the print preview shows the book as it will print, here a 6 by 9 inch page in EB Garamond with drop caps and a centred scene break.</figcaption>
        </Figure>
        <Figure class="plate">
          <div class="shot"><a href="./assets/design-work/hotpress-template-gallery.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="./assets/design-work/hotpress-template-gallery.webp" width="2880" height="1180" loading="lazy" alt="The Choose a Template gallery: typeset sample pages for Ashford, Pemberton, Thornbury, Whitmore, Cambridge and Hawthorne with their fonts named underneath, with the Serif filter selected among All, Serif, Sans, Script, Genre-Specific and Accessible." /></a></div>
          <figcaption>The template gallery shows each template as a typeset sample page with its fonts named underneath, and sorts the 22 into serif, sans, script, genre-specific and accessible.</figcaption>
        </Figure>
        <Figure class="plate">
          <div class="shot"><a href="./assets/design-work/hotpress-format-typography.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="./assets/design-work/hotpress-format-typography.webp" width="2880" height="1800" loading="lazy" alt="Hotpress Format Settings with the Typography section open: body font EB Garamond and heading font Playfair Display shown in their own typefaces, size and line height, and a preview of Chapter One, with Chapters, Page Layout and more sections below." /></a></div>
          <figcaption>Format settings are split into collapsible sections, and the font menus show each typeface in its own letterforms above a preview of heading and body text.</figcaption>
        </Figure>
        <Figure class="plate narrow">
          <div class="shot"><a href="./assets/design-work/hotpress-print-options.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="./assets/design-work/hotpress-print-options.webp" width="1150" height="1350" loading="lazy" alt="Hotpress print options: CMYK colour profile, 0.125 inch bleed, toggles for trim marks, registration marks and colour bars with a description under each, and a PDF/X-4 output intent, then EPUB options." /></a></div>
          <figcaption>The print options for CMYK colour, bleed, trim marks and a PDF/X-4 output intent use the same heavy-bordered inputs and toggles, with a one-line description under each toggle.</figcaption>
        </Figure>
        <Figure class="plate">
          <div class="shot"><a href="./assets/design-work/hotpress-thread.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="./assets/design-work/hotpress-thread.webp" width="1920" height="896" loading="lazy" alt="A commented phrase highlighted in yellow with a badge showing Ana's initial and two comments, and the Discussion popover open under it with Ana's comment, Swift Author's reply and a reply box." /></a></div>
          <figcaption>Comments attach to a highlighted phrase, with a badge showing the author's initial and the message count, and the thread opens in a yellow-headed popover under the text.</figcaption>
        </Figure>
        <ActionRow class="actions"><TextLink class="button" href="./hotpress.html">The case study and the video</TextLink>
          <TextLink class="button" href="https://hotpress.app">Open hotpress.app</TextLink></ActionRow>
      </WorkSection>

      <WorkSection class="work" id="koda">
        <h2><a href="./koda.html">Koda</a></h2>
        <MetaLine class="meta">Own project &middot; 2026 <Dot class="dot">&middot;</Dot> Desktop app</MetaLine>
        <p>Koda is a desktop app for working collaboratively with coding agents. I gave it the look of a terminal, with mostly monospaced type, thin frames and actions in brackets.</p>
        <Figure class="plate">
          <div class="shot dark"><a href="./assets/design-work/koda-two-macs.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="./assets/design-work/koda-two-macs.webp" width="3200" height="1786" loading="lazy" alt="One Koda session on two Macs side by side: Gorka's window in the black Obsidian theme and Alex's in the blue-grey Graphite theme, both showing the same agent transcript, with SHARED BY GORKA · CONTROL on Alex's window." /></a></div>
          <figcaption>This is one agent session on two Macs, with the host in Obsidian, the collaborator in Graphite and a badge on the collaborator's window reading Shared by Gorka.</figcaption>
        </Figure>
        <Figure class="plate narrow">
          <div class="shot dark"><a href="./assets/design-work/koda-empty-session.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="./assets/design-work/koda-empty-session.webp" width="700" height="480" loading="lazy" alt="The KODA TERMINAL v0.1.0 box with the KODA mark drawn in ASCII block characters and Ready for input, above Type a message or use /commands." /></a></div>
          <figcaption>An empty session opens on the KODA mark in ASCII inside a thin terminal frame.</figcaption>
        </Figure>
        <Figure class="plate">
          <div class="shot dark"><a href="./assets/design-work/koda-settings-themes.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="./assets/design-work/koda-settings-themes.webp" width="1440" height="525" loading="lazy" alt="Koda settings: a [THEME] section with four built-in theme cards, KODA Obsidian selected, Codex Light, KODA Dim and KODA Graphite, and an Import VS Code Theme button." /></a></div>
          <figcaption>There are four built-in themes, three dark and one light, with text contrast tested to WCAG AA, and a VS Code theme file can be imported and translated to Koda's design tokens.</figcaption>
        </Figure>
        <Figure class="plate">
          <div class="shot dark"><a href="./assets/design-work/koda-share-dialog.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="./assets/design-work/koda-share-dialog.webp" width="1280" height="760" loading="lazy" alt="Koda's Share Workspace dialog: a thin frame with the title between corner marks, an invitation link in a field ringed in orange, a [ DONE ] action and [ESC] CLOSE." /></a></div>
          <figcaption>The share dialog is a thin terminal frame with its title between corner marks, the invitation link in a field ringed in the orange accent, and [ DONE ] and [ESC] CLOSE as bracketed actions.</figcaption>
        </Figure>
        <ActionRow class="actions"><TextLink class="button" href="./koda.html">The case study and the video</TextLink>
          <TextLink class="button" href="https://gorkamolero.github.io/koda/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Koda's Storybook</TextLink></ActionRow>
      </WorkSection>

      <WorkSection class="work" id="openroadie">
        <h2><a href="https://github.com/RoadieHQ/openroadie" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>openroadie</em></a></h2>
        <MetaLine class="meta"><a href="https://roadie.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Roadie</a> &middot; 2026 <Dot class="dot">&middot;</Dot> Open source</MetaLine>
        <p><em>openroadie</em> is the open-source context layer that came out of Roadie, and I designed and built its interfaces. It maps how a company's services, repos and on-call connect, so agents can answer questions about them. The app runs on <code>@roadiehq/ui</code>, a design system I co-led, and these screens come from a local install with made-up demo data.</p>
        <Figure class="plate">
          <div class="shot dark"><a href="./assets/design-work/openroadie-pipeline-editor.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="./assets/design-work/openroadie-pipeline-editor.webp" width="2560" height="1600" loading="lazy" alt="The openroadie pipeline editor in the dark theme for a GitHub teams data source: schedule, source, two chained sources and store as cards joined by arrows, and after a dry run the selected step's JSON output in the panel on the right." /></a></div>
          <figcaption>Each pipeline is a column of step cards joined by arrows, and after a dry run you pick a step to read its output in the panel beside it.</figcaption>
        </Figure>
        <Figure class="plate">
          <div class="shot dark"><a href="./assets/design-work/openroadie-relationships-map.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="./assets/design-work/openroadie-relationships-map.webp" width="2560" height="1600" loading="lazy" alt="The openroadie relationships map in the dark theme, built with React Flow: nineteen data sources from GitHub, Okta, PagerDuty and Slack as cards with their join fields, linked by coloured and dashed lines, with a legend of relationship types and a minimap." /></a></div>
          <figcaption>Each data source is a card listing the fields it joins on, and each relationship is a line between two fields, coloured and dashed by type, with a legend.</figcaption>
        </Figure>
        <Figure class="plate">
          <div class="shot"><a href="./assets/design-work/openroadie-rule-editor.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="./assets/design-work/openroadie-rule-editor.webp" width="2560" height="1600" loading="lazy" alt="The openroadie rule editor for Okta user to GitHub user in the light theme: source, field email, the samePerson match and the target in one row, with ten generated pairs listed underneath, all matched." /></a></div>
          <figcaption>A relationship rule is one row, from source and field through the match to the target, with a preview of the pairs it generates underneath, ten of ten matched here.</figcaption>
        </Figure>
        <Carousel class="carousel" aria-label="The openroadie setup wizard in the terminal">
          <div class="carousel-track">
            <Figure class="slide"><div class="shot dark"><a href="./assets/design-work/openroadie-wizard-welcome.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="./assets/design-work/openroadie-wizard-welcome.webp" width="1950" height="1160" loading="lazy" alt="The openroadie setup wizard's welcome screen in the terminal, with its steps on the left and an orange heading." /></a></div><figcaption>Setup starts in the terminal, where you only pick integrations, add their secrets and connect them.</figcaption></Figure>
            <Figure class="slide"><div class="shot dark"><a href="./assets/design-work/openroadie-wizard-connect.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="./assets/design-work/openroadie-wizard-connect.webp" width="1950" height="1160" loading="lazy" alt="The setup wizard's connect step, with PagerDuty, GitHub, Okta and Slack each ticked green and 4/4 integrations connected above the list." /></a></div><figcaption>Each integration gets a green tick when it connects, with a running count above the list.</figcaption></Figure>
            <Figure class="slide"><div class="shot dark"><a href="./assets/design-work/openroadie-wizard-handoff.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="./assets/design-work/openroadie-wizard-handoff.webp" width="1950" height="1160" loading="lazy" alt="The setup wizard's last screen, with the command openroadie agent, the alternative skill install for a coding agent, and a note that tokens stay in setup." /></a></div><figcaption>On the last screen you hand over to an agent, with a command to run or a skill to give your own coding agent, and a note that your credentials stay in setup.</figcaption></Figure>
          </div>
          <div class="carousel-nav"><button type="button" class="carousel-prev" aria-label="Previous setup screen">&larr;</button><span class="carousel-count">1 / 3</span><button type="button" class="carousel-next" aria-label="Next setup screen">&rarr;</button></div>
        </Carousel>
        <Figure class="plate">
          <div class="shot"><a href="./assets/design-work/openroadie-onboarding-checklist.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="./assets/design-work/openroadie-onboarding-checklist.webp" width="2560" height="1600" loading="lazy" alt="The openroadie Getting started checklist in the light theme: a progress bar at four of six, the first four steps struck through as done, and a filled Create a context group button on the next step." /></a></div>
          <figcaption>The Getting started checklist shows progress as a bar, strikes through finished steps and gives only the next step a filled button.</figcaption>
        </Figure>
        <Figure class="plate">
          <div class="shot"><a href="./assets/design-work/openroadie-storybook-step-node.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="./assets/design-work/openroadie-storybook-step-node.webp" width="2700" height="2000" loading="lazy" alt="The StepNode component from @roadiehq/ui in Storybook, its In Flow story selected in the sidebar next to Default, Statuses and Disabled, showing a trigger, source and sink as connected cards." /></a></div>
          <figcaption>The pipeline step card is a <code>@roadiehq/ui</code> component, StepNode, with Default, Statuses, In Flow and Disabled stories in Storybook.</figcaption>
        </Figure>
        <ActionRow class="actions"><TextLink class="button" href="https://github.com/RoadieHQ/openroadie">The source on GitHub</TextLink></ActionRow>
      </WorkSection>

      <ActionRow class="actions"><TextLink class="button primary" href="./design-systems.html">Design systems</TextLink>
        <TextLink class="button" href="./index.html">Engineering portfolio</TextLink></ActionRow>
      </main>
  </PageFrame>
  <Fragment slot="after-footer" set:html={afterFooter} />
</Base>
